`useUndoRedoStack.push` closes over `cursor`, so multiple `push()` calls within the same React event can slice history using a stale cursor and drop intermediate snapshots. Consider updating `history` and `cursor` together via functional state (derive next cursor from previous cursor inside the setter) so push is safe under React 18 batching.
```suggestion
  const [state, setState] = useState<{ history: Snapshot[]; cursor: number }>({
    history: [initial],
    cursor: 0,
  });

  const { history, cursor } = state;
  const current = history[cursor];

  const push = useCallback((next: Snapshot) => {
    setState((prev) => {
      const nextHistory = [...prev.history.slice(0, prev.cursor + 1), next];
      return {
        history: nextHistory,
        cursor: nextHistory.length - 1,
      };
    });
  }, []);

  const undo = useCallback(() => {
    setState((prev) => ({
      ...prev,
      cursor: Math.max(0, prev.cursor - 1),
    }));
  }, []);

  const redo = useCallback(() => {
    setState((prev) => ({
      ...prev,
      cursor: Math.min(prev.history.length - 1, prev.cursor + 1),
    }));
```

`handleDragStart` sets `effectAllowed` but never calls `e.dataTransfer.setData(...)`. HTML5 DnD requires setting data in some browsers (notably Firefox), otherwise drag/drop may not start reliably. Set a dummy payload (or node id) in `dragstart`.
```suggestion
    }
    // Set a payload to ensure HTML5 drag-and-drop works reliably across browsers (e.g. Firefox)
    e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', nodeId);
```

`showMinimap`, `description`, and `setDescription` are currently unused, which suggests unfinished/unsupported schema fields. Either implement the minimap/description UI, or remove these fields from the destructure/state (and the schema/registry inputs) to avoid misleading API surface.
```suggestion
    showVersionHistory = false,
    showExecutionOverlay = false,
    executionSteps = [],
    versionHistory = [],
    concurrencyPolicy,
    title: initialTitle = 'New Flow',
    status: initialStatus = 'draft',
  } = schema;

  // ── State ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  const [title, setTitle] = useState(initialTitle);
```

This assertion isn’t verifying that a Task node was added: the left palette already renders a “Task” label before clicking, so `getAllByText('Task')` will pass even if `addNode` is broken. Consider asserting on something unique to the canvas node (e.g., count of `[role="group"]` nodes increases, or query by `aria-label` on the newly created node).
```suggestion
    const initialNodeCount = screen.getAllByRole('group').length;
    const addButton = screen.getByTitle('Add Task');
    fireEvent.click(addButton);
    const nodeCountAfterClick = screen.getAllByRole('group').length;
    expect(nodeCountAfterClick).toBeGreaterThan(initialNodeCount);
```
